    docs(install): agent-unique port + fresh-uptime probe guidance on shared-host installs (DF-OFF-BY-ONE-28)
    
    On bunker/shared-host installs the port is host-global and a nohup serve
    survives `bunker destroy`, so the next agent's install probes can hit a
    stale listener (observed: 54m uptime on las-bunker-03). Document the
    agent-unique port + kill-before-destroy rule and the consumer-side
    fresh-uptime /health assertion. Evidence:
    docs/dogfood/evidence/2026-10-02-install-bunker-las-03-raw.log
    
    Co-authored-by: Alexis Okuwa <wojonstech@gmail.com>
---
 README.md           | 2 ++
 docs/integration.md | 2 ++
 2 files changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 0026c4a5..b6c9493f 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -413,6 +413,8 @@ make build
 
 > **Port 8766 already in use?** `./off-by-one` binds `:8766` by default; if another instance (or any other service) already holds it, startup fails with `listen tcp :8766: bind: address already in use`. Run on an alternate port instead: `./off-by-one --port 18766` (or set `OFF_BY_ONE_PORT=18766` in `.env`) — see [Configuration](#configuration).
 
+> **Port hygiene on shared-host / bunker installs:** when agents share the host network namespace (e.g. bunker agents), the port is **host-global**, not agent-private. A `nohup ./off-by-one ... &` serve survives `bunker destroy` — nothing reaps agent-spawned background processes — so the next agent's fresh install can find a stale listener already holding "its" localhost port (observed: a 54-minute-uptime server answering the new install's probes, silently defeating install verification). Rule: pick an **agent-unique port** (not the default 8766) and **kill the serve before `bunker destroy`**. Consumer side: install-leg verification probes must assert the `/health` `uptime` is seconds (fresh), not just `status: ok` — a minutes-long uptime means you are talking to someone else's server. Evidence: [docs/dogfood/evidence/2026-10-02-install-bunker-las-03-raw.log](docs/dogfood/evidence/2026-10-02-install-bunker-las-03-raw.log).
+
 > **Rebuild after pulling:** the repo-root `./off-by-one` binary is gitignored (never committed) — after `git pull`, run `make build` and verify with `make check-binary-fresh` before running, so the binary matches HEAD instead of serving pre-fix behavior. A binary that came from a bare `go build` fails that check with `ERROR: ./off-by-one carries no version stamp (0.1.0-dev) — it was not built by 'make build'; run 'make build'` (and a binary built from a dirty tree, or one whose source has changed since it was built, fails the same way).
 
 > **Rebuild after ANY code commit (deploy rotation):** a Go commit is **not live**
diff --git a/docs/integration.md b/docs/integration.md
index b2c07128..50f5c45a 100644
--- a/docs/integration.md
+++ b/docs/integration.md
@@ -45,6 +45,8 @@ curl -s http://localhost:8766/health
 # {"status":"ok","uptime":"..."}
 ```
 
+> **Fresh-install probes:** assert the `uptime` is seconds (fresh), not just `status: ok`. On shared-host / bunker installs the port is host-global and an orphaned serve from a destroyed agent can still be bound — a long uptime means you are talking to a stale server, not the instance you just installed. See the README's "Port hygiene on shared-host / bunker installs" note (use an agent-unique port; kill the serve before `bunker destroy`).
